f hardware monitoring  assets for our atomic clocks and our  voltage crates  and a bunch of interaction design  objects which i'll take you through in  more detail  later this is like the purpose of this  is to like i said  monitor in real time for the sake of  debugging and troubleshooting everything  that's going on in this experiment  and so like i said i'll go back to these  skeumorphic detector representations  here  and so what we're looking at here is uh  duscant and duscont is  a collection of sensors it's a big round  thing actually which is why this is  round here and uh it detects um  neutrons uh neutrons firing pardon me  and so there are about 70  yeah there's 70 uh detectors or sensors  here  and at the bottom here we've got a scale  that shows from zero to one  kilohertz there yeah so all of the  colors that are sort of like the warm  range are firing between like 0.3 and  0.6 which is about 300 to 600  events per second now as bill was saying  before this information basically came  in a giant  like table that was a data dump so if  something ever went wrong with these  detectors  uh you would you know look in that table  and you would of course find probably a  zero  but the difficult thing with those table  dumps is the information was not always  necessarily organized  in a way where all of those zeroes might  come together as a clump  you would have to have domain knowledge  to know to look to that table  to see the zeros and then you have to go  through the table find all the zeros and  figure out  what that meant for where those were in  the detector like  which sensors were actually  malfunctioning by creating the  skeuomorphic representation of the  detector we can easily see at a glance  where the zeros are  and that this portion of the detector  actually has a problem now  the interesting thing about this is that  the context preserves a lot of  information it makes it a lot easier to  see what the problem is at a glance  now what i find fascinating about bill's  implementation here is  one day he came to me and he said  angelina can you help me debug my  javascript and i'd been teaching build  javascript before he had this job and i  was like of course sure  i will totally help you debug your  javascript and so i hadn't really  played too much with his web app i left  him to his own devices and i  uh you know right clicked on something  that i thought was going to be a dom  element and went to go inspect it in the  browser  and instead i found that bill had done  everything with like three canvases  so where i was expecting a bunch of dom  elements actually bill  had used a bunch of canvases and i asked  him i was like well you know i know you  understand the box model i know you  understand  html and css and javascript why did you  do everything in canvas  and the answer was very simple well you  know i was under some time pressures and  i just wanted to get something made  and i just didn't know how to make a  hexagonal div  i was like okay that seems reasonable um  and  uh and so he you know he discovered the  canvas object  and that he could control every pixel  written to the screen or in that canvas  object and that he could actually draw  out the skeuomorphic representation and  also deal with uh the changing state of  the experiment in a really elegant way  and on top of that you know so he  implemented this in canvas but he also  went on to implement his own  uh event listeners and also his own uh  tool tips as well  which on one hand i found totally  ridiculous and on the other hand i also  found totally amazing that like i just  kind of left him alone and this is what  he did  but this is a bit serendipitous right  like on one hand we can know know  in a situation that like if we leave  someone to the devices it might not turn  out terribly well  in bill's case uh with the number of  elements that he needs to represent  and their changing state doing this in  canvas probably in the end ends up being  more performant than juggling a bunch of  dom elements and dealing with their  state  so so for bill's purposes this ends up  being actually probably the better  solution  but you can imagine how like this could  have gone sideways where another  situation or another technology or  somebody just fumbling around and hoping  to get it right  um and so the interesting thing um in  addition to that  is let me pop over here to shark  we're dealing with so many channels of  information now this is an exploded view  of another detector of course it's got  the same sort of firing rate  representation  but actually these panels are uh  averages  of detectors within them so we've got  kind of like this long box-like thing  and then on the ends there's like these  circular things which is really  the technical way to explain it but if  you actually click  and drill down you can see that there is  actually even more detectors in there  so bill has done an excellent job here  of creating representation so that  scientists can get exactly the  information that they want and  knowing exactly what component in the  experiment is producing that information  without having to fumble with some  sort of table  okay so as angelina has walked through  here there's a whole lot of uh  information design problems we had to  address uh in griffin  and i mean there's still plenty more  right i mean doing a 70 channel detector  like descant like this was sort of no  big deal  when you move to something as complex as  shark i mean it's it's kind of tough to  tell what's going on here  someone with real experience in user  interface design could do even better  here  but anyway it's not just ui and ux  problems that  we have to deal with we also have a lot  of interaction problems to solve  so in any one of these experiments you  always have to set up  a simple experimental cycle that you're  going to run through while your  experiment runs  so how these experiments are typically  configured is there'll be a big  spherical array of detectors arranged  around a point where you're going to  want to have your source and how you put  your source in the middle of those  detectors is there's like a movie reel  like a movie tape  that you can move into place in the  middle of your detectors then you turn  your radioactive beam on and you implant  a bunch of  you know radioactive goop on that movie  tape then you turn your sensors on and  record data and you repeat that cycle  you know a million times for as long as  you want to collect data for  simple enough but before i developed  this web app  uh what you had to do to implement  something like that is that each of  those actions  would have like a little bit pattern and  then by hand for some reason  uh you had to take those bit patterns  and add them together turn it into a hex  number  take that hex number and put it in that  table  that big table dump of of instructions  that run this experiment  now i mean i suppose that's not very  complicated or very hard which is why it  survived for so long but it's also  completely unnecessary  there are so many good tools for  interacting with a  with a piece of software that being  stuck in the in the 1960s like that just  seems a little silly  so i mean what i developed to solve that  i can re-implement  the exact cycle i just described with  this interface i can clear my memory  there we go and i'll move my tape into  place and then maybe i'll implant some  radioactive source on my tape and then  i'll turn my detectors on  there we go i've made an i made an  experimental cycle for griffin it took  like 10 seconds  and all i had to do was use my intuitive  notion of  interactions that i've got from just you  know general fluency with  computers and smartphones and stuff no  special domain knowledge is required  anymore  that's one of the main goals for  interface design  in the sciences is to suppress that  domain knowledge put as much of it in  the background as possible  so we don't get distracted by it because  that's what domain knowledge ends up  being particularly for new collaborators  is a distraction that stops us from  making more progress in the sciences  all right so i'm going to talk a little  bit about the sort of cognitive biases  that really get us in the way of  ourselves and the way of achieving  things  so in 2012 i organized the vancouver  chapter of the  international space apps challenge and  so for the international space apps  challenge what nasa did was they said  we want to engage people who are not  necessarily professional scientists we  want people from interaction design  user experience design we want software  developers we want to get them together  for this weekend codeathon or hackathon  and we will pose a number of challenges  for them to solve  and then we would like to see the best  solutions you know the winners of each  one that  you know the judges thought were the  best and uh and then they you know they  would go on to sort of like an  international level of voting and  uh projects that were particularly  interesting and caught the eye of nasa  and they were released open source nasa  would then sort of like draw  either inspiration or use their code and  that happened in a number of instances  so in the vancouver location um we had a  fairly reasonable turnout for like a  code-a-thon hackathon kind of event  it was definitely skewed like the the  attendees are definitely  more skewed towards like academia like  we got a lot of people in computing  science and  uh disappointing for me not as many  people from the software industry  um one of the groups that one did a  really fantastic uh sorry the group that  one did a really fantastic  visualization of the aurora borealis in  real time using like a data feed from  nasa  and it was fantastic and when they were  talking to me they said  oh yeah you know last night i was  talking to my friends and they were  asking me all these questions about what  we were doing at the nasa space apps  challenge  i said oh what do your friends do  they're professional software developers  i'm like okay well  why didn't they come oh well when they  heard it was going to be science they  thought that they didn't have the  necessary skills or knowledge to  participate  and that was just really disappointing  for me because  even if you don't have the necessary  domain knowledge for physics that  doesn't mean that you can't  learn a little bit and it doesn't mean  that you can't contribute even if you  don't have specific domain knowledge  like bill does about all of these  detectors  i mean i obviously have a cursory  understanding now which which helps me  help bill  but you can still go through those code  examples earlier and see like that  there's obvious problems to be solved  so uh and then from the other end of  things uh often in physics  physicists or physicists and other  scientists can be very very resistant to  asking for outside help like when it  comes to things like third-party  libraries  um physicists like to have a lot of  control over the things that they're  doing  which is sort of why bill found the  canvas element and thought that was like  you know his sort of silver bullet  and the idea behind this is even if  they're building a big monolithic  unmaintainable piece of software  at least they know everything about it  so the idea of going to an open source  project  uh you know or or an established  solution for databases like you know  mysql or mongodb or something that in  the industry we would know is tried and  tested  uh the physicist or or other natural  scientist may have the impetus to be  like  well i don't want to put it in my code  if i don't understand everything about  it  so even suggesting like hey you're  building a web app why don't you use  like a selector library or why don't you  use  uh you know this this bit of niceness  they don't want to put it in their code  sometimes unless they can read through  every line  and and uh you know know how it works  and so they'll just avoid it and try  building their own thing  and i think it was bill that was telling  me about a particular instance in a  physics library where they had  decided not to use like a  production-ready database that we would  use in the industry but they'd  actually re-implemented their own  version of odbc  which was terrible so they're kind of  like repeating effort because uh there's  like this  this fear of uh taking up too much time  understanding these uh extra pieces  uh and it leads to to this i mean i  think that a lot of people that go  through their their phd and are in the  sciences end up kind of burnt out on a  lot of this code stuff because  they don't have any help and they don't  actually know any better and  really this is sort of contrary to the  idea that science should always be a  collaboration so why aren't software  developers collaborating with scientists  we've got this sort of barrier this idea  that  because we're not professional  scientists or maybe you know i'm a  software world because i'm not a  professional scientist  i can't contribute to science that's  patently false  and physicists and other natural  scientists that are you know  coding and learning to code should  probably be teaming up with people on  software so they can solve these  problems more  efficiently and do better science so the  question really is how do we adapt our  culture to do this with these  these sort of like cognitive biases in  play  this problem has to be approached from  both sides of the equation  actually and one of the most important  things is  for programmers is what angelina has  already touched on and that's that  uh the lack of domain knowledge in the  science of your choice whatever  interests you the most  does not matter at all we've got plenty  of physicists to handle that  and as you can see i mean angelina just  from some cursory conversations with me  picked up everything she needed to know  about the griffin experiment and its  auxiliary detectors  to be able to afford me the advice i  needed  working on this project we showed you  started off by showing you those code  examples and those uh  user interface and user experience uh  design examples  uh to emphasize the fact that really  what  often needs help with is things that are  very familiar to you and it's the domain  knowledge  not here but out there in this audience  and in the software  uh engineering field that's really uh  really at a loss for and we really need  uh to bring in  [Music]  so one of the most important things you  can do with your colleagues and friends  and  contacts in the sciences is educate them  on the value of  open sourcing their code there's very  little anyone outside the field can do  for scientists as long as their code  remains locked up in a black box  somewhere  on top of this encourage them to  consider the fact that  while they hold peer review very dear to  their hearts  when the overwhelming majority of their  results comes from code that no one can  see what does peer review really mean  at that point we need to start making  science more open  so that we can collaborate effectively  on it  if you want to get a little more  involved this is sort of like a grading  scale  of involvement and engagement the next  step would be code reviews  have a look once you get your scientist  friends to open source their code have a  look at it  see if you can spot any any  anti-patterns in it because as you can  see from the code examples we started  with  some of them are going to be really  obvious and really simple uh for  uh for professional software developers  it would be just a few minutes to look  at that and say hey  have you ever thought of a for loop or  have you ever thought of factoring that  into a function  and it would make a dramatic improvement  in this code  uh one step up from that as well would  be make a pull request refactor a little  bit of the code to do  things in a way that makes a lot more  sense from a good engineering  perspective  i mean we don't expect you to solve our  whole technical debt for us that's silly  but one tiny example that we can learn  from and take away  can make a tremendous difference  particularly in the life of a student  someone who's just learning just in grad  school now learning  how to code how to deal with these giant  frameworks of infrastructure  uh if they learn this early just with a  tiny example  uh they'll take that away for the rest  of their careers and that effect  ripples outwards um as you could see  from my uh  experiences in ui design as i mentioned  earlier i mean i have no training in  user experience obviously  normally when i interact with it it  feels a little bit like this  uh i tried my best but i'm sure there's  people out here that have even better  ideas than i did on how to make  physics experiments or experiments in  any field as interactable as possible  with a minimum of domain knowledge  another element that we could use help  with isn't just in software but in our  hardware infrastructure  so another uh sort of cutting edge  element of griffin  is that griffin will write 200 terabytes  of data to disk  every single week and we'd like to  analyze that  in a day now that's not an  insurmountable problem  but it's an ongoing discussion right now  of exactly how we're going to build  a cluster to do that and uh for some  people i mean this might be a problem  you've engaged with before  you might have some useful advice for us  i'd say we would love to  hear your expertise on these matters so  a message for scientists out there i  mean i know  that i know that earlier like you you  know if this is being recorded if you  heard the audience like giggle a little  bit  uh at the code they're like we're not  showing you this and we're not pointing  this out to shame anybody because  frankly  at some point when i was learning  software development and learning  programming uh years ago  i wrote code like that and obviously the  first example i showed you is code that  bill wrote and bill definitely writes  better code now so just to be clear like  there's this  isn't you know to shame anybody we just  want you to do better and  uh just understand that if you're not a  software engineer then you are out of  your domain  and you probably need to be accepting  help or looking for advice  as bill mentioned and we really want to  stress this get your code in the open  so much code is written for these  experiments these simulations to verify  results and it literally is a black box  people will submit these papers for  research uh for publication  and go to conferences and do poster  presentations but they don't show the  code with it  nobody looks at the code the supervisor  involved as long as the code at the end  spits out kind of the right looking  example kind of in the right looking  range  we just assume that it's okay and that's  a little bit of a problem i think  getting your code in the open really  really makes this a collaborative  process and it really makes science as  open as it should be  so bring software developers and  computing scientists on board and  preferably bring them on board early  because uh it will sort of have like a  ripple or a butterfly effect if you help  if you get some really good advice on  making design decisions with how you do  your software early on  and you can carry that through your  project and i mean if you're in a  situation at a lab where you're you know  you can't bring them on early bring them  on to help you manage the technical that  you that you have that's better than not  bringing them in  at all so then the other one is like i  was saying we're not trying to shame  anybody just be okay with the fact that  sometimes you write crap code  i know that when i'm prototyping  something like i'm prototyping a feature  i'll just be completely messy with it  i'll be like you know what i'm gonna  nest this like six times and i'm just  gonna kind of get a result  and then like two days day two days  later i go back to i'm like what idiot  wrote this  and i iterate on it and i make it better  and one of the things i want to mention  here is that over in the industry we've  sort of like you know  got this dichotomy of waterfall design  versus iterative  design or waterfall development versus  iterative development  and that's that you know in waterfall  development you like plan everything  linearly and you sort of expect that  everything's totally going to go  smoothly you're not going to hit any  roadblocks and thus you'll end up with a  perfect solution at the end  in reality this pretty much never  happens so i mean once you do get  something  working uh sort of you need to be aware  of the fact that probably your code  isn't going to go into a vacuum  somewhere even if you are reasonably  certain it is because your supervisor  might hand it off to somewhere else  to someone else and uh that you know  once you've written it just go back to  it and iterate on it and  and try and make it better don't don't  fall a victim to the notion that you  know you writing it once is going to be  enough for you that's just not how good  software is written  if you're a writer and you're writing a  novel you don't just go like okay first  draft  going to send it off to the publisher  guess that'll be okay  no no the editor will help you iterate  on the process  and then your book will be published the  same can definitely be true for software  because we like to pretend we all know  what we're doing when we're writing  software but really a lot of us just  fumble around kind of like this cat  so one particular philosophy i think  that could really really benefit  scientists out there especially when  people are learning to code is test  driven development or behavior driven  development  which if you're unfamiliar with it it  basically means that before you write  your main piece of software  you get a testing suite and there's some  available for all sorts of different  languages  and you write your tests first and you  describe the behavior that you want with  your tests  and then you write the software so that  those tests pass and this has a  effect in two ways one if somebody is  unfamiliar with how to write really good  software  you've kind of given them an itemized  list of the things that they need to  achieve you've kind of said okay if  you're going to write software to  support the simulation  then here's the things that you need to  do in order for things to  to be complete the other thing that  happens is when you do this it sort of  forces you to write really modular and  decoupled code  when you're testing very small pieces of  of  features or testing really really small  pieces of a library  your code by nature has to become more  modular and more modular code is  definitely more maintainable over time  so just to wrap up here  before i came to strange loop i talked  to a lot of different scientists not  just physicists because it's not just  physicists that have this problem  i spoke to agricultural scientists and  ecologists and  life scientists as many people as i  could speak to  and the story was the same from every  different field that i encountered  but the one anecdote that i wanted to  share that was the most important was  the one  from the ecologist that i spoke to i  asked the ecologist  what would you do with more computing  expertise on your team  and the ecologist said to me the most  useful thing that a software developer  could do for me today  would be just to sit down with me and  have a conversation  explain to me exactly what uh  software engineering can do for me our  fields have split  so much software engineering has  diverged so far  from so many of the natural sciences  sciences that professionals in the field  don't even know what to ask for at this  point and that technical debt  that ultimate cost of the extreme  example of reductionism that we've  gotten into  is starting to seriously hamper our  efforts to advance our sciences  we've gone very very far in our little  boxes  but now there's a tremendous amount of  low-hanging fruit  in the overlaps between our fields if we  want to start moving forward quickly  again  those are the places we have to look for  so when alex miller invited me to speak  at strange loop i actually  had not heard of strangeloop before and  i thought that was a bit surprising  because i speak at a lot of technical  conferences but  of course i you know typed it into my  you know generic search engine  and i looked up strange loop and i saw  some of the talks from last year and it  was immediately apparent to me what an  interdisciplinary conference this was  like there's clearly a lot of people  here from academic computing  there's a lot of people here from the  software industry and i knew that this  was the talk that i wanted to give and i  encouraged alex like come on i got to  bring dr mills with me because we've  been working on this for a while and we  really think that this is  obviously really important to help  better mankind and help uh you know  actually produce  better science with with reliable  results um  so i was really really glad that i could  come here today and share this with this  particular audience because i think that  you guys  uh may be probably the most receptive to  this idea  um so i mean things are changing uh a  project actually cropped up uh where at  cern right now a bunch of  professional web developers there's  actually some friends of mine there  brian leroux and remy sharp and  not pictured there's a really awesome  developer that i look up to named leah  veru  and they were actually brought into cern  to help recreate the original line  browser for the web  and although this isn't cern importing  people to necessarily work on a physics  experiment  they did go out of their way to say okay  we want to recreate this important piece  of web history  let's get people who are professionals  to do it and that's something i hope  that we see with labs and attitude more  and more  so this website  interdisciplinaryprogramming.com  tell your friends if you go there right  now there's a very very simple  web form um if you sign up at the web  form  all that happens is we're going to hold  on to your email and we're going to hold  into little information about like how  many people are scientists how many  people are software developers  and what bill and i are working on is a  platform to connect people in the  sciences  and connect people in software  engineering and computing science  together  so that in the future you know if you're  interested in helping science you can  like go to that website and be like you  know what i  want to work with someone doing like  quantum physics experiments oh look  there's a lab that might be receptive to  me helping the idea being that we're  kind of doing like a  matchmaking service for both sides so  that we can bridge this gap and that  project will be launching in 2014.  so please take this domain and tell your  friends tweet about it if you know  people that are in grad school  or working in an academic field please  tell them about it we would love to hear  from them especially about their  experiences and the kinds of things that  they would need  from a web platform like this so  in conclusion thank you strange loop for  having us here to sort of spread this  message  you can find me as angelina angelina  magnum on twitter and  bill is bill does physics because that  what he does and the slides will be  tweeted afterwards if you are interested  thanks everyone
 

